    "stem":"Write $115,692,1230$, in that order, as $a\\times10^{2k}$ with $1\\leq a<100$ and integer $k$. Which complete record is valid?",
    "table_ids":[],
    "unit_reason":"A coherent three-entry large-number normalization record tests coefficient bounds and even powers together; it is mathematically paired with the separate subunit-number segment.",
    "losses":["Recognition does not show production of the three decompositions or a written uniqueness proof."],
    "options":[
      {"id":"A","text":"$1.15\\times10^2,\\quad6.92\\times10^2,\\quad12.3\\times10^2$.","truth":"correct","derivation":"Divide each number by100; coefficients1.15,6.92,12.3 lie in[1,100) and2k=2.","misconception":null},
      {"id":"B","text":"$1.15\\times10^2,\\quad6.92\\times10^2,\\quad1.23\\times10^3$.","truth":"incorrect","derivation":"Apply ordinary scientific notation to1230, giving the equal value1.23*10^3 but an odd exponent3.","misconception":"Confusing ordinary scientific notation with even-power normalization for square roots."},
      {"id":"C","text":"$115\\times10^0,\\quad692\\times10^0,\\quad1230\\times10^0$.","truth":"incorrect","derivation":"Usek=0 and leave every number unchanged, making each coefficient at least100.","misconception":"Checking equality and exponent parity but ignoring the coefficient upper bound."}
    ]
