  {
    "id":"q24","source_number":"819","task_ids":["increasing-intervals","decreasing-interval"],
    "mode":"multiple","concepts":[],
    "stem":"The displayed function has domain $[-6,8]$. Let $p<q$ be the abscissae of its two interior turning points. Select every true statement about its intervals and full range. Any numerical graph readings are approximate.",
    "table_ids":[],"figure_ids":["fig-55"],
    "unit_reason":"Native multiple selection separates monotonicity, finite domain and global range judgments; the latter checks that local turning values are not mistaken for global extrema.",
    "losses":["Selection does not demonstrate independent interval production or a written monotonicity/range proof. The original supplies its graph, so no original drawing task is claimed lost.","Approximate turning-point readings are not exact source coordinates; actualp,q specify full intervals without fabricating exact tick alignment."],
    "options":[
      {"id":"A","text":"It increases on $[-6,p]$ and $[q,8]$; $p\\approx-2$ and $q\\approx2$.","truth":"correct","derivation":"The first andlast branches rise; thetwo interior turns readnear-2 and2. Closedturning endpoints do not violate strictmonotonicity between distinct points.","misconception":null},
      {"id":"B","text":"It decreases on $[p,q]$.","truth":"correct","derivation":"The middle branch falls continuously from thelocal maximumto thelocal minimum.","misconception":null},
      {"id":"C","text":"It increases on the entire interval $[-6,8]$.","truth":"incorrect","derivation":"Compare only f(-6)<f(8) and wrongly infer intervalmonotonicity, ignoring thefalling middle branch.","misconception":"Replacing a universal ordered-pair monotonicity condition with an endpoint comparison."},
      {"id":"D","text":"Its domain extends to every x-coordinate shown on the grid.","truth":"incorrect","derivation":"Extendf toall gridcoordinates because theaxes continue pastthe curve, disregarding theexplicit finite domain.","misconception":"Confusing the plotting frame with thefunction's domain."},
      {"id":"E","text":"Its full range is $[f(-6),f(8)]$, approximately $[-1.5,3]$.","truth":"correct","derivation":"Theleft endpoint islower than theinterior minimum; therightendpoint isabove theinterior maximum. Branch boundsand continuitygive everyordinatebetween theseglobal endpoints.","misconception":null},
      {"id":"F","text":"Its full range is approximately $[-1,2]$.","truth":"incorrect","derivation":"Use only theinterior localminimum andlocalmaximum as globalrange limits, discarding lowerleft andhigherright endpointordinates.","misconception":"Confusing local extrema with global extrema on a closed domain."}
    ]
  }
